## 2. Stakeholder Conflicts & Resolution Analysis
Conflict: Volunteer sign-up flexibility vs. organiser preparation time

Volunteer perspective: Volunteers may want to sign up at short notice.
Event Organiser perspective: Organisers may need time to finalise rosters, prepare equipment and check skills or experience.
Privacy / Security Officer perspective: Access to information about volunteers' skills or qualifications may need restrictions.

Resolution strategy: Different registration deadlines may be needed for different roles. The deadlines and the checking rules must be confirmed with organisers and the privacy role before they become requirements.

Open questions:
What is the latest acceptable registration time for each type of role
Who is responsible for checking volunteers' skills or qualifications
When must organisers finalise volunteer rosters

## 3. Candidate Requirements Catalogue
All requirements below are candidates: proposed — requires confirmation.

### 3.1 Functional Requirements
FR01 — Account Registration: The system shall allow a person to register as a volunteer and provide the required personal information (information to be confirmed).
FR02 — Volunteer Profile: The system shall allow a registered volunteer to record their skills and general availability.
FR03 — Skills Information: The system shall allow a registered volunteer to record the skills or experience they have (how this is checked is to be confirmed).
FR04 — Event Creation: The system shall allow an authorised organiser to create an event, specify the number of volunteers needed for each role, and record any skills or experience required for the role.
FR05 — Role Suitability: The system shall show an authorised organiser whether a volunteer's recorded skills match the requirements of a role (who checks them is to be confirmed).
FR06 — Notifications: The system shall notify volunteers registered for an event when it is cancelled or its details change.
FR07 — Attendance List: The system shall allow an authorised organiser to view the list of volunteers attending their event.

### 3.2 Non-Functional Requirements
NFR01 — Usability: At least 90% of first-time volunteers shall be able to search for and register for an event within 3 minutes without assistance. Proposed target. Check: usability test with representative volunteers.
NFR02 — Performance: Event searches shall return results within 2 seconds under normal peak use. Proposed target. Check: measure response times with an agreed number of concurrent users.
NFR03 — Security & Privacy: Volunteer contact details shall only be accessible to authorised users who need them for their responsibilities. Check: access-control test with different user accounts (who is authorised is to be confirmed).
NFR04 — Availability: The system shall be available for volunteer registration 99.5% of the time during agreed operational hours. Proposed target. Check: monitor availability over an agreed period.

## 4. Requirement Surgery

### Example 1
Raw stakeholder statement: We need a calendar that everyone can see so people know what's happening.

Analysis: The statement shows a need for volunteers to see upcoming events. "Calendar" is a possible solution, and "everyone" is ambiguous because different users may need different access.

Refined requirement: The system shall allow volunteers to view information about upcoming events that need volunteers.

Open question: Which event details should anyone be able to see, and which only registered volunteers?

Verification: Task-based walkthrough where a volunteer finds and views upcoming events.

## 5. Reflection
I learned that proposed solutions and business rules should not be treated as confirmed requirements without stakeholder agreement. Unclear deadlines, checking of skills and access permissions must be recorded as open questions and clarified through further elicitation.
